# Architectural heuristics

Applied when a decision is not settled by a rule.

## Where does this code belong

Ask what it decides. If it decides nothing and only moves data, it is infrastructure. If it decides whether or what, it is domain. If it only validates shape and hands off, it is transport.

"It is more convenient here" is not an argument. Convenience is how drift starts.

## Is this finding a problem

Not every finding is a defect. Duplication of email rendering and send-instruction publishers between services is the accepted price of autonomy here. Logger and tracing belong in `packages/observability`; that sharing is the intended platform library, not a clone to extract later. A finding is a problem when it moves the system away from its intended architecture, not merely when a tool reports it.

If a deviation is genuinely acceptable, record it as an accepted decision with a rationale rather than silencing the check.

## Adding a signal

A new signal earns its place if it can be computed deterministically and it says something the existing signals cannot. Otherwise it is noise with a weight attached.

## When speed pressures the design

The provider-bypass shortcut exists precisely because it is fast, works, and passes review. Speed is not design. If a change would be faster by crossing a boundary, that is the moment the boundary matters most.
